Selected API, matrix, lighting and display-list sources from src/ and
include/GL/gl.h. LICENSE preserves the archive's MIT notice, copyright
1997-2022 Fabrice Bellard. gl.h retains the attribution of constants to Mesa.

Local adaptations:
- Native Plan 9 libc, memory and fatal-error handling; mk and 6c -p.
  The -p option uses Plan 9's own ANSI preprocessor for the opcode table.
- No GLX, operating-system toolkit, software rasterizer or z-buffer code.
- virgl.c batches transformed vertices through libgpu and gpu(3). Matrices
  and vertex lighting run on the guest CPU; clipping, interpolation,
  rasterization and depth testing run through host VirGL. Frame readback
  returns top-down XRGB32 bytes; libdraw window policy stays in the app.
- Explicit rejection of unsupported operations; this is not conformant GL.
- Correct homogeneous transforms, matrix invalidation when lighting changes,
  general-axis rotation and frustum validation, flat provoking colors for
  assembled primitives, polygon winding, default normal/light/depth state,
  and cleanup of context-owned allocations.
- Filled convex polygons stream through a fixed four-vertex assembly buffer.
- Bounded display-list indices and nesting, nonzero generated list names,
  glDeleteLists, and validation of operations inside glBegin/glEnd. New lists
  remain private until glEndList; calls and deletion see the old definition.
  Reserved names are distinct from defined lists.
- Typed client arrays, indexed draws and buffer objects use byte strides
  and copy values into display lists; buffers remain in guest memory.
- Four texture units with owned images, pixel unpacking, mipmaps, filtering,
  texture matrices, coordinate generation and fixed-function combiners.
  Generated TGSI programs and sampler descriptors are cached per context.
  GPU texture copies are evicted by least recent use under resource pressure;
  retained CPU images allow transparent reupload and target resizing.
- Alpha testing/blending, depth masks/functions, polygon offset, two-sided
  materials, point size/attenuation and point sprites use VirGL state.
- Color masks, scissor rectangles and masked/scissored clears, line widths,
  wireframe/point polygons, texture subimages and pixel readback. Shared pixel
  conversion handles byte and packed 16-bit formats plus pack/unpack layout.
  Display lists own subimage data, and updates invalidate cached GPU copies.
- Correct scalar/vector argument lengths, material-list parameter ownership,
  linked light removal, local-viewer vector components and color-material
  tracking when enabled or reconfigured.

VirGL encoding derives from the existing generated9 examples and
virglrenderer at 4d8bd3c9954a9599e0de70a2e432c761da80398d.
See ../libgpu/UPSTREAM and ../libgpu/LICENSE for exact references and the
Red Hat MIT notice. LICENSE.virgl preserves that notice here as well.
New native integration and tests use the generated9 root LICENSE.

The native binding supports one current context per process and terminates
on errors, following TinyGL's error model. See tinygl(2) for limitations.
